<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Sacred · Celebration · c. 1050 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">Victimae paschali laudes</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="la">Victimæ paschali laudes</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A Latin song of joy for Easter Sunday, sung in church services since the 1000s.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>usually attributed to Wipo of Burgundy; also attributed to Notker Balbulus, Robert II of France and Adam of St. Victor</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Latin Christians of medieval Europe; today the Catholic, Anglican and Lutheran churches</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Latin</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>11th century</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Sacred · Celebration</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (Latin sequence of the 11th century, usually attributed to Wipo of Burgundy)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;Victimæ paschali laudes&quot; is a sequence, a sung text of the Mass, prescribed for Easter Sunday in the Catholic Mass and in Anglican and Lutheran Eucharistic services. It is usually attributed to Wipo of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>, an 11th-century chaplain to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-emperor">Holy Roman Emperor</a> Conrad II, but it has also been credited to Notker Balbulus, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-ii-of-france">Robert II of France</a> and to Adam of St. Victor.</p><p>Before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-trent">Council of Trent</a> (1545–1563) many feasts had sequences of their own, and some sixteen different sequences were in use for Easter alone. The Roman Missal of the Tridentine Mass, published in 1570 after the council, kept only four medieval sequences: this one, &quot;Veni Sancte Spiritus&quot; for Pentecost, &quot;Lauda Sion&quot; for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corpus-christi-feast">Corpus Christi</a> and &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dies-irae">Dies irae</a>&quot; for the Requiem Mass. A fifth, the &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stabat-mater">Stabat Mater</a>&quot;, was added by Pope Benedict XIII in 1727. &quot;Victimæ paschali laudes&quot; is one of the few sequences still in liturgical use today.</p><p>The 1570 missal also changed the text. It deleted a section with a pejorative reference to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, altered the wording of one line, and added &quot;Amen&quot; and &quot;Alleluia&quot; at the end.</p><p>Many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> composers set the text, among them Busnois, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josquin-des-prez">Josquin</a>, Lassus, Willaert, Hans Buchner, Palestrina, Byrd and Fernando de las Infantas; later settings include Perosi's and one for four-part choir and organ by the French composer Jehan Revert. German chorales grew from it: &quot;Christ ist erstanden&quot; in the 12th century and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a>'s &quot;Christ lag in Todes Banden&quot;. In English it is often sung in Jane E. Leeson's metrical paraphrase, to tunes such as Victimae Paschali and St George's Windsor or, with alleluias, Easter Hymn and Llanfair.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A song of praise for Easter Sunday, offered to Christ as the Easter sacrifice and celebrating his rising from the dead. The mood is solemn and joyful.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-blocks sg-blocks-2"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-1"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Latin</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Victimae paschali laudes</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">immolent Christiani.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Agnus redemit oves:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Christus innocens Patri</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">reconciliavit peccatores.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Mors et vita duello</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">conflixere mirando:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">dux vitae mortuus,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">regnat vivus.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Dic nobis Maria,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">quid vidisti in via?</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Sepulcrum Christi viventis,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">et gloriam vidi resurgentis:</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Angelicos testes,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">sudarium, et vestes.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Surrexit Christus spes mea:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">praecedet suos in Galilaeam.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Scimus Christum surrexisse</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">a mortuis vere:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Tu nobis, victor Rex, miserere.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Amen. Alleluia.</span></p></div></div><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-1"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Christ the Lord is risen today;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Christians, haste your vows to pay;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Offer ye your praises meet</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>At the Paschal Victim's feet.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>For the sheep the Lamb hath bled,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Sinless in the sinner's stead;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>&quot;Christ is risen,&quot; today we cry;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Now He lives no more to die.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Christ, the victim undefiled,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Man to God hath reconciled;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Whilst in strange and awful strife</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Met together Death and Life:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Christians, on this happy day</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Haste with joy your vows to pay;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>&quot;Christ is risen,&quot; today we cry;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Now He lives no more to die.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Say, O wondering Mary, say,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>what thou sawest on thy way.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>'I beheld, where Christ had lain,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>empty tomb and angels twain,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>I beheld the glory bright</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>of the rising Lord of light;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Christ my hope is risen again;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>now he lives, and lives to reign.'</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Christ, who once for sinners bled,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Now the first born from the dead,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Throned in endless might and power,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Lives and reigns forevermore.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Hail, eternal Hope on high!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Hail, Thou King of victory!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Hail, Thou Prince of life adored!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Help and save us, gracious Lord.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Attributed to Wipo of Burgundy (11th century); the Latin text as on Latin Wikisource. Text from Wikisource (<a href="https://la.wikisource.org/wiki/Victimae_paschali_laudes" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Victimae paschali laudes</a>); public domain.</p><p>English: Metrical paraphrase by Jane Eliza Leeson, as quoted in Wikipedia's article. Text from <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Victimae_paschali_laudes" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia</a>; public domain.</p></div></section>
</div>
